{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0113eeZMkQjmgzCA9HeBe8we","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"DocRED Confusability Study\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Count curve + BCS correlation on DocRED (96 relation types). Scaling stress test for document-level RE.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"count_1_acc\": 0.52,\n7\t      \"count_4_acc\": 0.48,\n8\t      \"count_8_acc\": 0.41,\n9\t      \"count_16_acc\": 0.34,\n10\t      \"count_32_acc\": 0.28,\n11\t      \"count_64_acc\": 0.22,\n12\t      \"count_96_acc\": 0.18,\n13\t      \"bcs_effect_count_8_delta_pp\": -14.5,\n14\t      \"bcs_effect_count_16_delta_pp\": -18.3,\n15\t      \"pairwise_r\": 0.09,\n16\t      \"pairwise_p\": 0.003,\n17\t      \"per_type_spearman_rho\": -0.15,\n18\t      \"per_type_p\": 0.11\n19\t    },\n20\t    \"tables\": [\n21\t      {\n22\t        \"name\": \"Table: DocRED Count Curve\",\n23\t        \"columns\": [\n24\t          \"count\",\n25\t          \"acc\",\n26\t          \"bcs_mean\",\n27\t          \"n_batches\"\n28\t        ],\n29\t        \"rows\": [\n30\t          [\n31\t            1,\n32\t            0.52,\n33\t            0.0,\n34\t            96\n35\t          ],\n36\t          [\n37\t            4,\n38\t            0.48,\n39\t            0.61,\n40\t            5\n41\t          ],\n42\t          [\n43\t            8,\n44\t            0.41,\n45\t            0.72,\n46\t            5\n47\t          ],\n48\t          [\n49\t            16,\n50\t            0.34,\n51\t            0.81,\n52\t            5\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            32,\n56\t            0.28,\n57\t            0.89,\n58\t            5\n59\t          ],\n60\t          [\n61\t            64,\n62\t            0.22,\n63\t            0.94,\n64\t            5\n65\t          ],\n66\t          [\n67\t            96,\n68\t            0.18,\n69\t            0.97,\n70\t            5\n71\t          ]\n72\t        ]\n73\t      },\n74\t      {\n75\t        \"name\": \"Table: DocRED BCS-Controlled\",\n76\t        \"columns\": [\n77\t          \"count\",\n78\t          \"low_bcs_acc\",\n79\t          \"high_bcs_acc\",\n80\t          \"delta_pp\"\n81\t        ],\n82\t        \"rows\": [\n83\t          [\n84\t            8,\n85\t            0.48,\n86\t            0.335,\n87\t            -14.5\n88\t          ],\n89\t          [\n90\t            16,\n91\t            0.43,\n92\t            0.247,\n93\t            -18.3\n94\t          ]\n95\t        ]\n96\t      }\n97\t    ],\n98\t    \"figures\": [],\n99\t    \"analysis\": \"DocRED replicates the confusability effect cross-task (sentence RE → document RE) but with higher noise floor. BCS effect amplification from 16-rel to 96-rel (est. 2.0x vs FewRel's 3.4x) is weaker because document-level errors are multi-causal. Usable as supporting evidence in Table but not a primary result. The weaker pairwise signal (r=0.09) is consistent with the median tier expectation that cross-task generalization holds directionally but not uniformly. Report as \\\"consistent direction, attenuated by document-level complexity\\\" in paper.\\n\\n\\nDocRED is document-level RE with multi-hop reasoning, inherently harder than FewRel sentence-level. Baseline accuracy lower (~52% at count_1 vs FewRel's 86%) because document-level context window is larger and entity pair identification adds complexity. Count degradation steeper in absolute terms (52%→18%) because 96 relation types create a denser confusability space than 16-rel FewRel, and document-level prompts consume more context budget. BCS effect present (-14 to -18pp) but noisier than FewRel because document-level errors conflate relation confusion with entity pair misidentification. Pairwise r≈0.09 is weaker than Few-NERD (0.156) due to document-level noise floor — many errors stem from entity linking failures rather than pure type confusion. Per-type signal directional but not significant (p=0.11) because DocRED has fewer high-frequency relation types (many long-tail types with <5 instances in test set).\\n\"\n100\t  },\n101\t  {\n102\t    \"name\": \"Interaction Effect 2x2\",\n103\t    \"description\": \"{low count, high count} × {low BCS, high BCS} factorial on Few-NERD. Tests additive vs multiplicative interaction.\",\n104\t    \"results\": {\n105\t      \"low_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.38,\n106\t      \"low_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.31,\n107\t      \"high_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.32,\n108\t      \"high_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.21,\n109\t      \"count_main_effect_f1\": -0.08,\n110\t      \"bcs_main_effect_f1\": -0.09,\n111\t      \"interaction_term_f1\": -0.02,\n112\t      \"interaction_p\": 0.14,\n113\t      \"model\": \"two-way ANOVA\"\n114\t    },\n115\t    \"tables\": [\n116\t      {\n117\t        \"name\": \"Table: 2x2 Factorial Results (F1)\",\n118\t        \"columns\": [\n119\t          \"\",\n120\t          \"Low BCS (≤Q1)\",\n121\t          \"High BCS (≥Q3)\"\n122\t        ],\n123\t        \"rows\": [\n124\t          [\n125\t            \"Low count (6)\",\n126\t            \"0.38 ± 0.04\",\n127\t            \"0.31 ± 0.05\"\n128\t          ],\n129\t          [\n130\t            \"High count (22)\",\n131\t            \"0.32 ± 0.03\",\n132\t            \"0.21 ± 0.04\"\n133\t          ]\n134\t        ]\n135\t      },\n136\t      {\n137\t        \"name\": \"Table: ANOVA Summary\",\n138\t        \"columns\": [\n139\t          \"Source\",\n140\t          \"SS\",\n141\t          \"F\",\n142\t          \"p\"\n143\t        ],\n144\t        \"rows\": [\n145\t          [\n146\t            \"Count\",\n147\t            0.042,\n148\t            8.7,\n149\t            0.004\n150\t          ],\n151\t          [\n152\t            \"BCS\",\n153\t            0.051,\n154\t            10.6,\n155\t            0.002\n156\t          ],\n157\t          [\n158\t            \"Count × BCS\",\n159\t            0.008,\n160\t            1.7,\n161\t            0.14\n162\t          ],\n163\t          [\n164\t            \"Residual\",\n165\t            0.245,\n166\t            \"\",\n167\t            \"\"\n168\t          ]\n169\t        ]\n170\t      }\n171\t    ],\n172\t    \"figures\": [],\n173\t    \"analysis\": \"Additive model explains >85% of variance from count and BCS. Interaction term trending but not significant — underpowered. Paper frames as: \\\"Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels; we find no evidence of significant interaction (p=0.14), consistent with an additive multi-channel model.\\\" The non-significant interaction actually strengthens the multi-channel narrative by supporting channel independence.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, count and BCS degrade F1 through partially independent channels (recall vs type confusion), so main effects are both significant. The interaction term is negative (super-additive: high count + high BCS is worse than sum of individual effects) but NOT significant at p<0.05 because: (a) n=27 batches per cell is underpowered for detecting interaction, (b) the interaction is modest (-2pp) relative to noise in batch-level metrics. This is actually fine for the paper — additive model is simpler to present and the slight super-additivity is mentioned as a trend. The 2x2 table still provides clean visual evidence that both channels operate independently.\\n\"\n174\t  },\n175\t  {\n176\t    \"name\": \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN\",\n177\t    \"description\": \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN 168 event types. Compare vs single-pass + random batching.\",\n178\t    \"results\": {\n179\t      \"single_pass_relaxed_f1\": 0.245,\n180\t      \"single_pass_precision\": 0.28,\n181\t      \"single_pass_recall\": 0.22,\n182\t      \"sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1\": 0.17,\n183\t      \"sdgd_zero_precision\": 0.12,\n184\t      \"sdgd_zero_recall\": 0.29,\n185\t      \"random_batch_relaxed_f1\": 0.19,\n186\t      \"random_batch_precision\": 0.14,\n187\t      \"random_batch_recall\": 0.3,\n188\t      \"per_type_relaxed_f1\": 0.15,\n189\t      \"per_type_precision\": 0.09,\n190\t      \"per_type_recall\": 0.41,\n191\t      \"sdgd_zero_vs_single_delta_f1\": -0.075,\n192\t      \"n_sdgd_batches\": 12,\n193\t      \"sdgd_time_s\": 720,\n194\t      \"single_pass_time_s\": 95\n195\t    },\n196\t    \"tables\": [\n197\t      {\n198\t        \"name\": \"Table: MAVEN Decomposition Comparison (relaxed F1)\",\n199\t        \"columns\": [\n200\t          \"Method\",\n201\t          \"F1\",\n202\t          \"Precision\",\n203\t          \"Recall\",\n204\t          \"Batches\",\n205\t          \"Time(s)\"\n206\t        ],\n207\t        \"rows\": [\n208\t          [\n209\t            \"Single-pass\",\n210\t            0.245,\n211\t            0.28,\n212\t            0.22,\n213\t            1,\n214\t            95\n215\t          ],\n216\t          [\n217\t            \"SDGD-zero\",\n218\t            0.17,\n219\t            0.12,\n220\t            0.29,\n221\t            12,\n222\t            720\n223\t          ],\n224\t          [\n225\t            \"Random-batch\",\n226\t            0.19,\n227\t            0.14,\n228\t            0.3,\n229\t            12,\n230\t            680\n231\t          ],\n232\t          [\n233\t            \"Per-type\",\n234\t            0.15,\n235\t            0.09,\n236\t            0.41,\n237\t            168,\n238\t            4200\n239\t          ]\n240\t        ]\n241\t      }\n242\t    ],\n243\t    \"figures\": [],\n244\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-task confirmation that merge amplification is the fundamental bottleneck, not confusability-aware batching. On MAVEN, SDGD-zero even closer to random-batch (Δ=0.02 vs 0.07 on Few-NERD), consistent with homogeneous schema reducing the benefit of similarity-based separation. Paper uses this as evidence that merge amplification is \\\"task-general\\\" and not an artifact of NER-specific evaluation.\\n\\n\\nMAVEN replicates the exp-008 merge amplification pattern: all decomposition modes F1 < single-pass. The effect is even more pronounced on MAVEN because: (1) 168 types means more batches (12 vs 8), amplifying merge conflicts, (2) event detection triggers are shorter spans (often single words) making merge deduplication harder — same trigger word extracted as different event types across batches, (3) MAVEN's semantically homogeneous type space means SDGD-zero's embedding-based separation is less effective (types that look dissimilar in embedding space may still be confused by the LLM due to context overlap). Random batching performs similarly to SDGD-zero (gap narrows from Few-NERD) because in a homogeneous schema, there's no \\\"good\\\" partition — all batches have similar internal confusability. This cross-task replication of the negative result strengthens the merge amplification narrative.\\n\"\n245\t  },\n246\t  {\n247\t    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\",\n248\t    \"description\": \"Empirical confusability from 5-10 calibration examples, no gradients. Compare vs SDGD-zero.\",\n249\t    \"results\": {\n250\t      \"sdgd_cal_f1\": 0.3,\n251\t      \"sdgd_cal_precision\": 0.24,\n252\t      \"sdgd_cal_recall\": 0.4,\n253\t      \"sdgd_zero_f1\": 0.2514,\n254\t      \"single_pass_f1\": 0.4451,\n255\t      \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta\": 0.05,\n256\t      \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta\": -0.145,\n257\t      \"calibration_cost_examples\": 8,\n258\t      \"calibration_cost_api_calls\": 8,\n259\t      \"empirical_vs_embedding_confusion_rank_corr\": 0.38\n260\t    },\n261\t    \"tables\": [\n262\t      {\n263\t        \"name\": \"Table: SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero vs Single-pass\",\n264\t        \"columns\": [\n265\t          \"Method\",\n266\t          \"F1\",\n267\t          \"P\",\n268\t          \"R\",\n269\t          \"Batches\",\n270\t          \"Calibration\"\n271\t        ],\n272\t        \"rows\": [\n273\t          [\n274\t            \"Single-pass\",\n275\t            0.445,\n276\t            0.563,\n277\t            0.368,\n278\t            1,\n279\t            \"N/A\"\n280\t          ],\n281\t          [\n282\t            \"SDGD-zero\",\n283\t            0.251,\n284\t            0.21,\n285\t            0.314,\n286\t            8,\n287\t            \"None\"\n288\t          ],\n289\t          [\n290\t            \"SDGD-cal\",\n291\t            0.3,\n292\t            0.24,\n293\t            0.4,\n294\t            8,\n295\t            \"8 examples\"\n296\t          ],\n297\t          [\n298\t            \"Random-batch\",\n299\t            0.32,\n300\t            0.249,\n301\t            0.446,\n302\t            8,\n303\t            \"N/A\"\n304\t          ]\n305\t        ]\n306\t      }\n307\t    ],\n308\t    \"figures\": [],\n309\t    \"analysis\": \"SDGD-cal demonstrates that better confusability estimation helps batch composition (+5pp over SDGD-zero) but cannot overcome merge amplification. Notably, random-batch still beats SDGD-cal in F1 (0.32 vs 0.30) because random batching doesn't systematically concentrate difficult types — it achieves better precision through lucky mixing. This is the smoking gun for \\\"the problem is merge, not partition.\\\" Paper frames SDGD-cal as ablation evidence isolating the merge bottleneck.\\n\\n\\nCalibration improves confusability estimation (empirical confusion matrix is more accurate than embedding cosine similarity — rank corr ≈0.38 vs perfect). SDGD-cal creates better batch partitions than SDGD-zero: fewer high-confusion pairs co-batched. This yields +5pp F1 over SDGD-zero (better precision from fewer intra-batch confusions). However, SDGD-cal still loses to single-pass by 14.5pp because merge amplification is independent of batch quality — it occurs at the merge step regardless of how well-separated the batches are. Recall still higher than single-pass (0.40 vs 0.37) confirming decomposition's recall benefit, but precision gap (0.24 vs 0.56) dominates. The calibration cost is minimal (8 API calls) but the gain doesn't overcome the fundamental merge problem. In median tier, this is a marginal improvement that confirms the bottleneck diagnosis rather than solving it.\\n\"\n310\t  },\n311\t  {\n312\t    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN\",\n313\t    \"description\": \"Empirical confusability with calibration on 168 event types. Full comparison table.\",\n314\t    \"results\": {\n315\t      \"sdgd_cal_relaxed_f1\": 0.19,\n316\t      \"sdgd_cal_precision\": 0.14,\n317\t      \"sdgd_cal_recall\": 0.32,\n318\t      \"sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1\": 0.17,\n319\t      \"single_pass_relaxed_f1\": 0.245,\n320\t      \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta\": 0.02,\n321\t      \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta\": -0.055,\n322\t      \"calibration_cost_examples\": 10\n323\t    },\n324\t    \"tables\": [\n325\t      {\n326\t        \"name\": \"Table: MAVEN Full Comparison\",\n327\t        \"columns\": [\n328\t          \"Method\",\n329\t          \"Relaxed F1\",\n330\t          \"P\",\n331\t          \"R\",\n332\t          \"Batches\"\n333\t        ],\n334\t        \"rows\": [\n335\t          [\n336\t            \"Single-pass\",\n337\t            0.245,\n338\t            0.28,\n339\t            0.22,\n340\t            1\n341\t          ],\n342\t          [\n343\t            \"SDGD-zero\",\n344\t            0.17,\n345\t            0.12,\n346\t            0.29,\n347\t            12\n348\t          ],\n349\t          [\n350\t            \"SDGD-cal\",\n351\t            0.19,\n352\t            0.14,\n353\t            0.32,\n354\t            12\n355\t          ],\n356\t          [\n357\t            \"Random-batch\",\n358\t            0.19,\n359\t            0.14,\n360\t            0.3,\n361\t            12\n362\t          ],\n363\t          [\n364\t            \"Per-type\",\n365\t            0.15,\n366\t            0.09,\n367\t            0.41,\n368\t            168\n369\t          ]\n370\t        ]\n371\t      }\n372\t    ],\n373\t    \"figures\": [],\n374\t    \"analysis\": \"MAVEN confirms merge amplification is task-general. SDGD-cal ≈ random-batch on MAVEN (both 0.19), reinforcing that partition quality is secondary to merge quality. Paper presents the full 2-task × 5-method table as Table 4, with merge amplification as the unified explanation for all decomposition failures.\\n\\n\\nOn MAVEN, SDGD-cal's improvement over SDGD-zero is smaller (+2pp vs +5pp on Few-NERD) because: (1) the semantically homogeneous schema means even empirical calibration struggles to find clean partitions — most type pairs have non-trivial confusability, (2) 168 types require 12+ batches, amplifying merge issues further, (3) event trigger boundaries are inherently fuzzy, so merge deduplication errors compound. The gap between SDGD-cal and single-pass (-5.5pp) is smaller than Few-NERD (-14.5pp) in relative terms because MAVEN single-pass baseline is already lower (0.245 vs 0.445). Pattern is consistent: calibration helps confusability estimation but merge amplification remains the binding constraint.\\n\"\n375\t  },\n376\t  {\n377\t    \"name\": \"LLaMA Multi-Model Validation\",\n378\t    \"description\": \"Replicate exp-002 protocol on Few-NERD with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct. Addresses single-model concern.\",\n379\t    \"results\": {\n380\t      \"pairwise_pearson_r\": 0.08,\n381\t      \"pairwise_pearson_p\": 0.0004,\n382\t      \"pairwise_n_pairs\": 1953,\n383\t      \"pairwise_spearman_rho\": 0.07,\n384\t      \"pairwise_spearman_p\": 0.002,\n385\t      \"nonzero_spearman_rho\": 0.15,\n386\t      \"nonzero_spearman_p\": 0.012,\n387\t      \"per_type_spearman_rho\": -0.18,\n388\t      \"per_type_p\": 0.08,\n389\t      \"count_6_f1\": 0.28,\n390\t      \"count_12_f1\": 0.31,\n391\t      \"confusion_ratio_extreme_count_6\": 4.2,\n392\t      \"confusion_ratio_extreme_count_12\": 5.8,\n393\t      \"bcs_effect_count_12_delta_f1\": -0.06,\n394\t      \"f1_vs_bcs_partial_r_count_12\": 0.32,\n395\t      \"f1_vs_bcs_partial_p_count_12\": 0.04\n396\t    },\n397\t    \"tables\": [\n398\t      {\n399\t        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\",\n400\t        \"columns\": [\n401\t          \"Metric\",\n402\t          \"Qwen3-32B\",\n403\t          \"LLaMA-3.3-70B\"\n404\t        ],\n405\t        \"rows\": [\n406\t          [\n407\t            \"Pairwise r\",\n408\t            0.156,\n409\t            0.08\n410\t          ],\n411\t          [\n412\t            \"Pairwise p\",\n413\t            \"4.6e-12\",\n414\t            \"4e-4\"\n415\t          ],\n416\t          [\n417\t            \"Nonzero ρ\",\n418\t            0.254,\n419\t            0.15\n420\t          ],\n421\t          [\n422\t            \"Per-type ρ\",\n423\t            -0.293,\n424\t            -0.18\n425\t          ],\n426\t          [\n427\t            \"Per-type p\",\n428\t            0.02,\n429\t            0.08\n430\t          ],\n431\t          [\n432\t            \"Confusion ratio (extreme)\",\n433\t            \"10-28x\",\n434\t            \"4-6x\"\n435\t          ]\n436\t        ]\n437\t      },\n438\t      {\n439\t        \"name\": \"Table: LLaMA Count Curve\",\n440\t        \"columns\": [\n441\t          \"count\",\n442\t          \"F1\",\n443\t          \"Precision\",\n444\t          \"Recall\"\n445\t        ],\n446\t        \"rows\": [\n447\t          [\n448\t            1,\n449\t            0.3,\n450\t            0.2,\n451\t            0.58\n452\t          ],\n453\t          [\n454\t            6,\n455\t            0.28,\n456\t            0.22,\n457\t            0.39\n458\t          ],\n459\t          [\n460\t            12,\n461\t            0.31,\n462\t            0.3,\n463\t            0.32\n464\t          ],\n465\t          [\n466\t            22,\n467\t            0.27,\n468\t            0.31,\n469\t            0.24\n470\t          ],\n471\t          [\n472\t            44,\n473\t            0.35,\n474\t            0.4,\n475\t            0.31\n476\t          ],\n477\t          [\n478\t            64,\n479\t            0.33,\n480\t            0.45,\n481\t            0.26\n482\t          ]\n483\t        ]\n484\t      }\n485\t    ],\n486\t    \"figures\": [],\n487\t    \"analysis\": \"LLaMA replicates direction on all metrics but with roughly half the effect size. Pairwise signal remains significant (p<0.001) — sufficient for \\\"consistent direction across models.\\\" Per-type p=0.08 means we cannot claim per-type significance on LLaMA alone, but combined with Qwen3 the pattern is clear. Paper softens multi-model claim to \\\"consistent direction across models, strongest on primary model (Qwen3-32B)\\\" and notes embedding-model alignment as a factor. This is adequate for EMNLP Main with the softened claim; a reviewer requesting LLaMA-specific embeddings for BCS is addressable in rebuttal but not pre-submission given timeline.\\n\\n\\nLLaMA-3.3-70B shows directionally consistent but weaker signal than Qwen3-32B because: (1) LLaMA's instruction-following for structured NER output is less reliable — more format errors and missed entities reduce statistical power by adding noise uncorrelated with BCS, (2) LLaMA-70B has more capacity than Qwen3-32B, potentially better at distinguishing similar types (higher baseline → smaller confusability effect), (3) the Qwen3 embeddings used for BCS computation are naturally more predictive of Qwen3's confusion patterns than LLaMA's — there's an embedding-model alignment effect (documented in D010 for MiniLM vs Qwen3). Pairwise r=0.08 is significant (p<0.001, n=1953) but half the Qwen3 effect (0.156). Per-type analysis trends negative (ρ=-0.18) but p=0.08 misses significance — consistent with median tier definition \\\"directional but not compelling standalone.\\\" Confusion ratio 4-6x is above 1 but well below Qwen3's 10-28x, because LLaMA's higher capacity means extreme batches still partially resolve confusable types. The cross-model BCS effect delta is -6pp (vs Qwen3's implied ~-8pp at count_12), falling in the defined range [-20, -5].\\n\"\n488\t  },\n489\t  {\n490\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Random Batching\",\n491\t    \"description\": \"Random batching with same k as SDGD. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\",\n492\t    \"results\": {\n493\t      \"random_k8_f1\": 0.315,\n494\t      \"random_k8_precision\": 0.245,\n495\t      \"random_k8_recall\": 0.44,\n496\t      \"random_k8_std_f1\": 0.025,\n497\t      \"sdgd_zero_k8_f1\": 0.251,\n498\t      \"single_pass_f1\": 0.445,\n499\t      \"random_vs_sdgd_delta\": 0.064,\n500\t      \"random_vs_single_delta\": -0.13,\n501\t      \"n_random_seeds\": 10,\n502\t      \"random_seed_f1_range\": [\n503\t        0.28,\n504\t        0.35\n505\t      ]\n506\t    },\n507\t    \"tables\": [\n508\t      {\n509\t        \"name\": \"Table: Random Batching Ablation (10 seeds)\",\n510\t        \"columns\": [\n511\t          \"Seed\",\n512\t          \"F1\",\n513\t          \"P\",\n514\t          \"R\"\n515\t        ],\n516\t        \"rows\": [\n517\t          [\n518\t            \"mean\",\n519\t            0.315,\n520\t            0.245,\n521\t            0.44\n522\t          ],\n523\t          [\n524\t            \"std\",\n525\t            0.025,\n526\t            0.02,\n527\t            0.03\n528\t          ],\n529\t          [\n530\t            \"min\",\n531\t            0.28,\n532\t            0.21,\n533\t            0.39\n534\t          ],\n535\t          [\n536\t            \"max\",\n537\t            0.35,\n538\t            0.28,\n539\t            0.49\n540\t          ]\n541\t        ]\n542\t      },\n543\t      {\n544\t        \"name\": \"Table: Decomposition Method Ranking (Few-NERD)\",\n545\t        \"columns\": [\n546\t          \"Method\",\n547\t          \"F1\",\n548\t          \"ΔF1 vs single\"\n549\t        ],\n550\t        \"rows\": [\n551\t          [\n552\t            \"Single-pass\",\n553\t            0.445,\n554\t            \"—\"\n555\t          ],\n556\t          [\n557\t            \"Random-batch (k=8)\",\n558\t            0.315,\n559\t            -0.13\n560\t          ],\n561\t          [\n562\t            \"Per-type (k=66)\",\n563\t            0.292,\n564\t            -0.153\n565\t          ],\n566\t          [\n567\t            \"SDGD-zero (k=8)\",\n568\t            0.251,\n569\t            -0.194\n570\t          ]\n571\t        ]\n572\t      }\n573\t    ],\n574\t    \"figures\": [],\n575\t    \"analysis\": \"Random > SDGD-zero is paradoxical if you assume confusability-aware partition should help. Paper explains: SDGD-zero optimizes for one objective (minimize intra-batch confusability) but ignores another (maintain contextual coherence). The real bottleneck is post-merge deduplication, which degrades proportionally to batch count regardless of batch composition. This motivates framing SDGD's failure as revealing merge amplification rather than as a partition failure.\\n\\n\\nRandom batching at k=8 outperforms SDGD-zero (+6.4pp F1), replicating exp-008's finding. This occurs because SDGD-zero's confusability-minimizing partition concentrates semantically distant types together — these types often span different domains (e.g., person subtypes + location subtypes), and the model performs worse when batch types are too heterogeneous (loses cross-type contextual priming). Random batches accidentally achieve better balance between similarity separation and contextual coherence. However, random batching still loses to single-pass by 13pp because merge amplification affects all decomposition equally. The 10-seed variance (σ=0.025) shows random batching is moderately stable. In median tier, this ablation is the key evidence that batch composition quality is NOT the bottleneck — merge strategy is.\\n\"\n576\t  },\n577\t  {\n578\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Per-Type Splitting\",\n579\t    \"description\": \"UniNER-style 1 type per batch. Upper bound on decomposition recall, reveals latency-quality tradeoff.\",\n580\t    \"results\": {\n581\t      \"per_type_f1\": 0.292,\n582\t      \"per_type_precision\": 0.194,\n583\t      \"per_type_recall\": 0.586,\n584\t      \"per_type_batches\": 66,\n585\t      \"per_type_time_s\": 2532,\n586\t      \"per_type_recall_vs_single\": 0.218,\n587\t      \"per_type_precision_vs_single\": -0.369,\n588\t      \"per_type_f1_vs_single\": -0.153,\n589\t      \"per_type_unique_preds\": 8450,\n590\t      \"single_pass_unique_preds\": 3200,\n591\t      \"merge_dup_rate\": 0.38\n592\t    },\n593\t    \"tables\": [\n594\t      {\n595\t        \"name\": \"Table: Per-Type Detailed Breakdown\",\n596\t        \"columns\": [\n597\t          \"Metric\",\n598\t          \"Single-pass\",\n599\t          \"Per-type\",\n600\t          \"Delta\"\n601\t        ],\n602\t        \"rows\": [\n603\t          [\n604\t            \"F1\",\n605\t            0.445,\n606\t            0.292,\n607\t            -0.153\n608\t          ],\n609\t          [\n610\t            \"Precision\",\n611\t            0.563,\n612\t            0.194,\n613\t            -0.369\n614\t          ],\n615\t          [\n616\t            \"Recall\",\n617\t            0.368,\n618\t            0.586,\n619\t            0.218\n620\t          ],\n621\t          [\n622\t            \"Unique preds\",\n623\t            3200,\n624\t            8450,\n625\t            \"+164%\"\n626\t          ],\n627\t          [\n628\t            \"Merge duplicates\",\n629\t            \"N/A\",\n630\t            \"38%\",\n631\t            \"\"\n632\t          ],\n633\t          [\n634\t            \"Time (s)\",\n635\t            77,\n636\t            2532,\n637\t            \"33x\"\n638\t          ]\n639\t        ]\n640\t      }\n641\t    ],\n642\t    \"figures\": [],\n643\t    \"analysis\": \"Per-type confirms the merge amplification mechanism: recall scales with decomposition granularity but precision degrades faster. The Pareto frontier shows no decomposition point dominates single-pass on F1. This is the strongest evidence for \\\"merge amplification as fundamental bottleneck\\\" — even the oracle partition (zero confusability) cannot beat single-pass without a better merge strategy. Paper uses per-type as the upper bound in the decomposition analysis table.\\n\\n\\nPer-type splitting eliminates ALL type confusion (zero intra-batch confusability) and maximizes recall (0.586 vs single-pass 0.368, +21.8pp). But precision collapses (0.194 vs 0.563, -36.9pp) because: (1) each batch extracts aggressively for its one type — without competing types, the model over-predicts, (2) merging 66 batch outputs creates massive duplication — the same span extracted as different types in different batches, (3) merge heuristics (longest match, confidence-based) cannot reliably disambiguate since each batch's prediction is locally confident. The 38% merge duplicate rate quantifies this directly. Per-type is the theoretical upper bound for recall under decomposition but the worst-case for precision. The 33x latency overhead (2532s vs 77s) makes it impractical regardless. This is already in exp-008 metrics; exp-014 adds systematic analysis of WHY precision drops (merge duplication rate, over-prediction per batch).\\n\"\n644\t  },\n645\t  {\n646\t    \"name\": \"SCS Validation\",\n647\t    \"description\": \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy.\",\n648\t    \"results\": {\n649\t      \"spearman_rho_qwen3\": 0.35,\n650\t      \"spearman_p_qwen3\": 0.0001,\n651\t      \"kendall_tau_qwen3\": 0.27,\n652\t      \"spearman_rho_minilm\": 0.28,\n653\t      \"spearman_p_minilm\": 0.001,\n654\t      \"top10_overlap_qwen3\": 0.5,\n655\t      \"top10_overlap_minilm\": 0.4,\n656\t      \"n_pairs_with_empirical\": 420\n657\t    },\n658\t    \"tables\": [\n659\t      {\n660\t        \"name\": \"Table: SCS vs Empirical Confusion Rank Correlation\",\n661\t        \"columns\": [\n662\t          \"Embedding\",\n663\t          \"Spearman ρ\",\n664\t          \"p\",\n665\t          \"Kendall τ\",\n666\t          \"Top-10 overlap\"\n667\t        ],\n668\t        \"rows\": [\n669\t          [\n670\t            \"Qwen3-32B\",\n671\t            0.35,\n672\t            \"1e-4\",\n673\t            0.27,\n674\t            \"5/10\"\n675\t          ],\n676\t          [\n677\t            \"MiniLM-L6-v2\",\n678\t            0.28,\n679\t            \"1e-3\",\n680\t            0.21,\n681\t            \"4/10\"\n682\t          ]\n683\t        ]\n684\t      },\n685\t      {\n686\t        \"name\": \"Table: Top Confused Pairs — Empirical vs Predicted\",\n687\t        \"columns\": [\n688\t          \"Rank\",\n689\t          \"Empirical top pair\",\n690\t          \"Emp. confusion\",\n691\t          \"SCS rank\"\n692\t        ],\n693\t        \"rows\": [\n694\t          [\n695\t            1,\n696\t            \"person-other ↔ person-politician\",\n697\t            156,\n698\t            2\n699\t          ],\n700\t          [\n701\t            2,\n702\t            \"person-other ↔ person-artist\",\n703\t            98,\n704\t            5\n705\t          ],\n706\t          [\n707\t            3,\n708\t            \"person-athlete ↔ person-other\",\n709\t            87,\n710\t            3\n711\t          ],\n712\t          [\n713\t            4,\n714\t            \"location-GPE ↔ location-bodiesofwater\",\n715\t            45,\n716\t            18\n717\t          ],\n718\t          [\n719\t            5,\n720\t            \"building-library ↔ building-other\",\n721\t            41,\n722\t            7\n723\t          ]\n724\t        ]\n725\t      }\n726\t    ],\n727\t    \"figures\": [],\n728\t    \"analysis\": \"ρ=0.35 validates SCS as a directionally useful proxy — significantly better than random (p<0.001) but insufficient for precise partition optimization. The asymmetry insight (top empirical pair person-other↔politician at SCS rank 2, but location-GPE↔ bodiesofwater at SCS rank 18) suggests type-name semantics capture intra-domain confusion well but miss cross-domain false positives. Paper presents this as motivation for calibration-based approaches and as explaining why SDGD-zero underperforms SDGD-cal. Also explains the D010 embedding sensitivity finding — different embeddings capture different aspects of confusability.\\n\\n\\nEmbedding SCS is a moderate proxy for empirical confusion (ρ=0.35 for Qwen3, 0.28 for MiniLM). The correlation is significant but far from perfect because: (1) embedding similarity captures semantic relatedness, not confusion — \\\"person-artist\\\" and \\\"person-athlete\\\" are semantically similar but the model may distinguish them well from context, (2) empirical confusion is asymmetric (model confuses A→B more than B→A) while cosine similarity is symmetric, (3) empirical confusion is heavily influenced by type frequency and context patterns not captured by type-name embeddings. Qwen3 embeddings outperform MiniLM (ρ=0.35 vs 0.28) on Few-NERD because of extraction-model alignment (consistent with D010 findings). Top-10 overlap at 50% means half the most-confused empirical pairs are also highest-SCS — useful but not sufficient for precision. This validates SCS as a reasonable zero-shot proxy while motivating calibration-based approaches (SDGD-cal). In median tier, this is adequate evidence for \\\"embedding SCS is a useful but imperfect confusability proxy.\\\"\\n\"\n729\t  },\n730\t  {\n731\t    \"name\": \"SDGD v2 on Few-NERD\",\n732\t    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 with merge amplification mitigation (confidence thresholding, duplicate suppression). Tests whether fixing merge recovers SDGD benefit.\",\n733\t    \"results\": {\n734\t      \"sdgd_v2_f1\": 0.42,\n735\t      \"sdgd_v2_precision\": 0.44,\n736\t      \"sdgd_v2_recall\": 0.4,\n737\t      \"sdgd_v2_vs_single_delta_f1\": -0.025,\n738\t      \"sdgd_v2_vs_zero_delta_f1\": 0.169,\n739\t      \"sdgd_v2_precision_drop_vs_single\": -0.123,\n740\t      \"merge_dup_rate_v2\": 0.12,\n741\t      \"merge_dup_rate_v1\": 0.38,\n742\t      \"confidence_threshold\": 0.7\n743\t    },\n744\t    \"tables\": [\n745\t      {\n746\t        \"name\": \"Table: SDGD v2 vs All Methods\",\n747\t        \"columns\": [\n748\t          \"Method\",\n749\t          \"F1\",\n750\t          \"P\",\n751\t          \"R\",\n752\t          \"Merge dup%\",\n753\t          \"Time(s)\"\n754\t        ],\n755\t        \"rows\": [\n756\t          [\n757\t            \"Single-pass\",\n758\t            0.445,\n759\t            0.563,\n760\t            0.368,\n761\t            \"N/A\",\n762\t            77\n763\t          ],\n764\t          [\n765\t            \"SDGD-zero\",\n766\t            0.251,\n767\t            0.21,\n768\t            0.314,\n769\t            \"38%\",\n770\t            409\n771\t          ],\n772\t          [\n773\t            \"SDGD v2\",\n774\t            0.42,\n775\t            0.44,\n776\t            0.4,\n777\t            \"12%\",\n778\t            450\n779\t          ],\n780\t          [\n781\t            \"Random-batch\",\n782\t            0.32,\n783\t            0.249,\n784\t            0.446,\n785\t            \"35%\",\n786\t            476\n787\t          ]\n788\t        ]\n789\t      }\n790\t    ],\n791\t    \"figures\": [],\n792\t    \"analysis\": \"SDGD v2 demonstrates that merge amplification IS the bottleneck (fixing it recovers most of the F1 gap) but the fix is incomplete. F1 delta of -2.5pp vs single-pass means SDGD v2 is not cost-effective (5x latency for -2.5pp). Paper pivots: SDGD's primary contribution is diagnostic (merge amplification) rather than practical (improved extraction). The v2 result becomes evidence for \\\"merge amplification is partially addressable but fundamentally limits naive decomposition approaches.\\\" This explains why UniNER and LLM-PEE report modest, inconsistent gains — they also face merge amplification but don't characterize it.\\n\\nSDGD v2 partially mitigates merge amplification through confidence-based deduplication and span-level voting across batches. Merge duplicate rate drops from 38% to 12%. Precision recovers substantially (0.44 vs SDGD-zero's 0.21) but still below single-pass (0.56) because: (1) confidence calibration is imperfect — the model's confidence doesn't reliably distinguish correct from incorrect type assignments, (2) some legitimate multi-type spans get suppressed by dedup, reducing recall slightly, (3) without explicit negative examples per batch, the model still over-extracts for the types it sees. Net F1 is 0.42, within -2.5pp of single-pass — a massive improvement over SDGD-zero but not a clear win. In median tier, this is \\\"marginal or negative\\\" — the delta is within noise for a method that costs 5x more API calls. Paper frames as: \\\"merge mitigation partially recovers decomposition benefit but does not achieve the positive result needed to claim SDGD as a practical method.\\\"\\n\"\n793\t  }\n794\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3403e61f-04f1-4371-bd33-f97dec98dbcd","uuid":"2453c720-4e21-4071-85d6-d27eeef84636","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:28:15.542Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"DocRED Confusability Study\",\n    \"description\": \"Count curve + BCS correlation on DocRED (96 relation types). Scaling stress test for document-level RE.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"count_1_acc\": 0.52,\n      \"count_4_acc\": 0.48,\n      \"count_8_acc\": 0.41,\n      \"count_16_acc\": 0.34,\n      \"count_32_acc\": 0.28,\n      \"count_64_acc\": 0.22,\n      \"count_96_acc\": 0.18,\n      \"bcs_effect_count_8_delta_pp\": -14.5,\n      \"bcs_effect_count_16_delta_pp\": -18.3,\n      \"pairwise_r\": 0.09,\n      \"pairwise_p\": 0.003,\n      \"per_type_spearman_rho\": -0.15,\n      \"per_type_p\": 0.11\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: DocRED Count Curve\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"acc\",\n          \"bcs_mean\",\n          \"n_batches\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.52,\n            0.0,\n            96\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            0.48,\n            0.61,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            8,\n            0.41,\n            0.72,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            16,\n            0.34,\n            0.81,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            32,\n            0.28,\n            0.89,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            64,\n            0.22,\n            0.94,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            96,\n            0.18,\n            0.97,\n            5\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: DocRED BCS-Controlled\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"low_bcs_acc\",\n          \"high_bcs_acc\",\n          \"delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            8,\n            0.48,\n            0.335,\n            -14.5\n          ],\n          [\n            16,\n            0.43,\n            0.247,\n            -18.3\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DocRED replicates the confusability effect cross-task (sentence RE → document RE) but with higher noise floor. BCS effect amplification from 16-rel to 96-rel (est. 2.0x vs FewRel's 3.4x) is weaker because document-level errors are multi-causal. Usable as supporting evidence in Table but not a primary result. The weaker pairwise signal (r=0.09) is consistent with the median tier expectation that cross-task generalization holds directionally but not uniformly. Report as \\\"consistent direction, attenuated by document-level complexity\\\" in paper.\\n\\n\\nDocRED is document-level RE with multi-hop reasoning, inherently harder than FewRel sentence-level. Baseline accuracy lower (~52% at count_1 vs FewRel's 86%) because document-level context window is larger and entity pair identification adds complexity. Count degradation steeper in absolute terms (52%→18%) because 96 relation types create a denser confusability space than 16-rel FewRel, and document-level prompts consume more context budget. BCS effect present (-14 to -18pp) but noisier than FewRel because document-level errors conflate relation confusion with entity pair misidentification. Pairwise r≈0.09 is weaker than Few-NERD (0.156) due to document-level noise floor — many errors stem from entity linking failures rather than pure type confusion. Per-type signal directional but not significant (p=0.11) because DocRED has fewer high-frequency relation types (many long-tail types with <5 instances in test set).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Interaction Effect 2x2\",\n    \"description\": \"{low count, high count} × {low BCS, high BCS} factorial on Few-NERD. Tests additive vs multiplicative interaction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"low_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.38,\n      \"low_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.31,\n      \"high_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.32,\n      \"high_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.21,\n      \"count_main_effect_f1\": -0.08,\n      \"bcs_main_effect_f1\": -0.09,\n      \"interaction_term_f1\": -0.02,\n      \"interaction_p\": 0.14,\n      \"model\": \"two-way ANOVA\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 2x2 Factorial Results (F1)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"Low BCS (≤Q1)\",\n          \"High BCS (≥Q3)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Low count (6)\",\n            \"0.38 ± 0.04\",\n            \"0.31 ± 0.05\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"High count (22)\",\n            \"0.32 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.21 ± 0.04\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: ANOVA Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Source\",\n          \"SS\",\n          \"F\",\n          \"p\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Count\",\n            0.042,\n            8.7,\n            0.004\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BCS\",\n            0.051,\n            10.6,\n            0.002\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Count × BCS\",\n            0.008,\n            1.7,\n            0.14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Residual\",\n            0.245,\n            \"\",\n            \"\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Additive model explains >85% of variance from count and BCS. Interaction term trending but not significant — underpowered. Paper frames as: \\\"Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels; we find no evidence of significant interaction (p=0.14), consistent with an additive multi-channel model.\\\" The non-significant interaction actually strengthens the multi-channel narrative by supporting channel independence.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, count and BCS degrade F1 through partially independent channels (recall vs type confusion), so main effects are both significant. The interaction term is negative (super-additive: high count + high BCS is worse than sum of individual effects) but NOT significant at p<0.05 because: (a) n=27 batches per cell is underpowered for detecting interaction, (b) the interaction is modest (-2pp) relative to noise in batch-level metrics. This is actually fine for the paper — additive model is simpler to present and the slight super-additivity is mentioned as a trend. The 2x2 table still provides clean visual evidence that both channels operate independently.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD-zero on MAVEN 168 event types. Compare vs single-pass + random batching.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"single_pass_relaxed_f1\": 0.245,\n      \"single_pass_precision\": 0.28,\n      \"single_pass_recall\": 0.22,\n      \"sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1\": 0.17,\n      \"sdgd_zero_precision\": 0.12,\n      \"sdgd_zero_recall\": 0.29,\n      \"random_batch_relaxed_f1\": 0.19,\n      \"random_batch_precision\": 0.14,\n      \"random_batch_recall\": 0.3,\n      \"per_type_relaxed_f1\": 0.15,\n      \"per_type_precision\": 0.09,\n      \"per_type_recall\": 0.41,\n      \"sdgd_zero_vs_single_delta_f1\": -0.075,\n      \"n_sdgd_batches\": 12,\n      \"sdgd_time_s\": 720,\n      \"single_pass_time_s\": 95\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: MAVEN Decomposition Comparison (relaxed F1)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"Precision\",\n          \"Recall\",\n          \"Batches\",\n          \"Time(s)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-pass\",\n            0.245,\n            0.28,\n            0.22,\n            1,\n            95\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-zero\",\n            0.17,\n            0.12,\n            0.29,\n            12,\n            720\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random-batch\",\n            0.19,\n            0.14,\n            0.3,\n            12,\n            680\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-type\",\n            0.15,\n            0.09,\n            0.41,\n            168,\n            4200\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-task confirmation that merge amplification is the fundamental bottleneck, not confusability-aware batching. On MAVEN, SDGD-zero even closer to random-batch (Δ=0.02 vs 0.07 on Few-NERD), consistent with homogeneous schema reducing the benefit of similarity-based separation. Paper uses this as evidence that merge amplification is \\\"task-general\\\" and not an artifact of NER-specific evaluation.\\n\\n\\nMAVEN replicates the exp-008 merge amplification pattern: all decomposition modes F1 < single-pass. The effect is even more pronounced on MAVEN because: (1) 168 types means more batches (12 vs 8), amplifying merge conflicts, (2) event detection triggers are shorter spans (often single words) making merge deduplication harder — same trigger word extracted as different event types across batches, (3) MAVEN's semantically homogeneous type space means SDGD-zero's embedding-based separation is less effective (types that look dissimilar in embedding space may still be confused by the LLM due to context overlap). Random batching performs similarly to SDGD-zero (gap narrows from Few-NERD) because in a homogeneous schema, there's no \\\"good\\\" partition — all batches have similar internal confusability. This cross-task replication of the negative result strengthens the merge amplification narrative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\",\n    \"description\": \"Empirical confusability from 5-10 calibration examples, no gradients. Compare vs SDGD-zero.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_cal_f1\": 0.3,\n      \"sdgd_cal_precision\": 0.24,\n      \"sdgd_cal_recall\": 0.4,\n      \"sdgd_zero_f1\": 0.2514,\n      \"single_pass_f1\": 0.4451,\n      \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta\": 0.05,\n      \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta\": -0.145,\n      \"calibration_cost_examples\": 8,\n      \"calibration_cost_api_calls\": 8,\n      \"empirical_vs_embedding_confusion_rank_corr\": 0.38\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero vs Single-pass\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"P\",\n          \"R\",\n          \"Batches\",\n          \"Calibration\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-pass\",\n            0.445,\n            0.563,\n            0.368,\n            1,\n            \"N/A\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-zero\",\n            0.251,\n            0.21,\n            0.314,\n            8,\n            \"None\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-cal\",\n            0.3,\n            0.24,\n            0.4,\n            8,\n            \"8 examples\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random-batch\",\n            0.32,\n            0.249,\n            0.446,\n            8,\n            \"N/A\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SDGD-cal demonstrates that better confusability estimation helps batch composition (+5pp over SDGD-zero) but cannot overcome merge amplification. Notably, random-batch still beats SDGD-cal in F1 (0.32 vs 0.30) because random batching doesn't systematically concentrate difficult types — it achieves better precision through lucky mixing. This is the smoking gun for \\\"the problem is merge, not partition.\\\" Paper frames SDGD-cal as ablation evidence isolating the merge bottleneck.\\n\\n\\nCalibration improves confusability estimation (empirical confusion matrix is more accurate than embedding cosine similarity — rank corr ≈0.38 vs perfect). SDGD-cal creates better batch partitions than SDGD-zero: fewer high-confusion pairs co-batched. This yields +5pp F1 over SDGD-zero (better precision from fewer intra-batch confusions). However, SDGD-cal still loses to single-pass by 14.5pp because merge amplification is independent of batch quality — it occurs at the merge step regardless of how well-separated the batches are. Recall still higher than single-pass (0.40 vs 0.37) confirming decomposition's recall benefit, but precision gap (0.24 vs 0.56) dominates. The calibration cost is minimal (8 API calls) but the gain doesn't overcome the fundamental merge problem. In median tier, this is a marginal improvement that confirms the bottleneck diagnosis rather than solving it.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN\",\n    \"description\": \"Empirical confusability with calibration on 168 event types. Full comparison table.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_cal_relaxed_f1\": 0.19,\n      \"sdgd_cal_precision\": 0.14,\n      \"sdgd_cal_recall\": 0.32,\n      \"sdgd_zero_relaxed_f1\": 0.17,\n      \"single_pass_relaxed_f1\": 0.245,\n      \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_delta\": 0.02,\n      \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_delta\": -0.055,\n      \"calibration_cost_examples\": 10\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: MAVEN Full Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Relaxed F1\",\n          \"P\",\n          \"R\",\n          \"Batches\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-pass\",\n            0.245,\n            0.28,\n            0.22,\n            1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-zero\",\n            0.17,\n            0.12,\n            0.29,\n            12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-cal\",\n            0.19,\n            0.14,\n            0.32,\n            12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random-batch\",\n            0.19,\n            0.14,\n            0.3,\n            12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-type\",\n            0.15,\n            0.09,\n            0.41,\n            168\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"MAVEN confirms merge amplification is task-general. SDGD-cal ≈ random-batch on MAVEN (both 0.19), reinforcing that partition quality is secondary to merge quality. Paper presents the full 2-task × 5-method table as Table 4, with merge amplification as the unified explanation for all decomposition failures.\\n\\n\\nOn MAVEN, SDGD-cal's improvement over SDGD-zero is smaller (+2pp vs +5pp on Few-NERD) because: (1) the semantically homogeneous schema means even empirical calibration struggles to find clean partitions — most type pairs have non-trivial confusability, (2) 168 types require 12+ batches, amplifying merge issues further, (3) event trigger boundaries are inherently fuzzy, so merge deduplication errors compound. The gap between SDGD-cal and single-pass (-5.5pp) is smaller than Few-NERD (-14.5pp) in relative terms because MAVEN single-pass baseline is already lower (0.245 vs 0.445). Pattern is consistent: calibration helps confusability estimation but merge amplification remains the binding constraint.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LLaMA Multi-Model Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate exp-002 protocol on Few-NERD with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct. Addresses single-model concern.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"pairwise_pearson_r\": 0.08,\n      \"pairwise_pearson_p\": 0.0004,\n      \"pairwise_n_pairs\": 1953,\n      \"pairwise_spearman_rho\": 0.07,\n      \"pairwise_spearman_p\": 0.002,\n      \"nonzero_spearman_rho\": 0.15,\n      \"nonzero_spearman_p\": 0.012,\n      \"per_type_spearman_rho\": -0.18,\n      \"per_type_p\": 0.08,\n      \"count_6_f1\": 0.28,\n      \"count_12_f1\": 0.31,\n      \"confusion_ratio_extreme_count_6\": 4.2,\n      \"confusion_ratio_extreme_count_12\": 5.8,\n      \"bcs_effect_count_12_delta_f1\": -0.06,\n      \"f1_vs_bcs_partial_r_count_12\": 0.32,\n      \"f1_vs_bcs_partial_p_count_12\": 0.04\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Qwen3-32B\",\n          \"LLaMA-3.3-70B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pairwise r\",\n            0.156,\n            0.08\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Pairwise p\",\n            \"4.6e-12\",\n            \"4e-4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Nonzero ρ\",\n            0.254,\n            0.15\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-type ρ\",\n            -0.293,\n            -0.18\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-type p\",\n            0.02,\n            0.08\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Confusion ratio (extreme)\",\n            \"10-28x\",\n            \"4-6x\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: LLaMA Count Curve\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"Precision\",\n          \"Recall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.3,\n            0.2,\n            0.58\n          ],\n          [\n            6,\n            0.28,\n            0.22,\n            0.39\n          ],\n          [\n            12,\n            0.31,\n            0.3,\n            0.32\n          ],\n          [\n            22,\n            0.27,\n            0.31,\n            0.24\n          ],\n          [\n            44,\n            0.35,\n            0.4,\n            0.31\n          ],\n          [\n            64,\n            0.33,\n            0.45,\n            0.26\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"LLaMA replicates direction on all metrics but with roughly half the effect size. Pairwise signal remains significant (p<0.001) — sufficient for \\\"consistent direction across models.\\\" Per-type p=0.08 means we cannot claim per-type significance on LLaMA alone, but combined with Qwen3 the pattern is clear. Paper softens multi-model claim to \\\"consistent direction across models, strongest on primary model (Qwen3-32B)\\\" and notes embedding-model alignment as a factor. This is adequate for EMNLP Main with the softened claim; a reviewer requesting LLaMA-specific embeddings for BCS is addressable in rebuttal but not pre-submission given timeline.\\n\\n\\nLLaMA-3.3-70B shows directionally consistent but weaker signal than Qwen3-32B because: (1) LLaMA's instruction-following for structured NER output is less reliable — more format errors and missed entities reduce statistical power by adding noise uncorrelated with BCS, (2) LLaMA-70B has more capacity than Qwen3-32B, potentially better at distinguishing similar types (higher baseline → smaller confusability effect), (3) the Qwen3 embeddings used for BCS computation are naturally more predictive of Qwen3's confusion patterns than LLaMA's — there's an embedding-model alignment effect (documented in D010 for MiniLM vs Qwen3). Pairwise r=0.08 is significant (p<0.001, n=1953) but half the Qwen3 effect (0.156). Per-type analysis trends negative (ρ=-0.18) but p=0.08 misses significance — consistent with median tier definition \\\"directional but not compelling standalone.\\\" Confusion ratio 4-6x is above 1 but well below Qwen3's 10-28x, because LLaMA's higher capacity means extreme batches still partially resolve confusable types. The cross-model BCS effect delta is -6pp (vs Qwen3's implied ~-8pp at count_12), falling in the defined range [-20, -5].\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Random Batching\",\n    \"description\": \"Random batching with same k as SDGD. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"random_k8_f1\": 0.315,\n      \"random_k8_precision\": 0.245,\n      \"random_k8_recall\": 0.44,\n      \"random_k8_std_f1\": 0.025,\n      \"sdgd_zero_k8_f1\": 0.251,\n      \"single_pass_f1\": 0.445,\n      \"random_vs_sdgd_delta\": 0.064,\n      \"random_vs_single_delta\": -0.13,\n      \"n_random_seeds\": 10,\n      \"random_seed_f1_range\": [\n        0.28,\n        0.35\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Random Batching Ablation (10 seeds)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Seed\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"P\",\n          \"R\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"mean\",\n            0.315,\n            0.245,\n            0.44\n          ],\n          [\n            \"std\",\n            0.025,\n            0.02,\n            0.03\n          ],\n          [\n            \"min\",\n            0.28,\n            0.21,\n            0.39\n          ],\n          [\n            \"max\",\n            0.35,\n            0.28,\n            0.49\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Decomposition Method Ranking (Few-NERD)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"ΔF1 vs single\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-pass\",\n            0.445,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random-batch (k=8)\",\n            0.315,\n            -0.13\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-type (k=66)\",\n            0.292,\n            -0.153\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-zero (k=8)\",\n            0.251,\n            -0.194\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Random > SDGD-zero is paradoxical if you assume confusability-aware partition should help. Paper explains: SDGD-zero optimizes for one objective (minimize intra-batch confusability) but ignores another (maintain contextual coherence). The real bottleneck is post-merge deduplication, which degrades proportionally to batch count regardless of batch composition. This motivates framing SDGD's failure as revealing merge amplification rather than as a partition failure.\\n\\n\\nRandom batching at k=8 outperforms SDGD-zero (+6.4pp F1), replicating exp-008's finding. This occurs because SDGD-zero's confusability-minimizing partition concentrates semantically distant types together — these types often span different domains (e.g., person subtypes + location subtypes), and the model performs worse when batch types are too heterogeneous (loses cross-type contextual priming). Random batches accidentally achieve better balance between similarity separation and contextual coherence. However, random batching still loses to single-pass by 13pp because merge amplification affects all decomposition equally. The 10-seed variance (σ=0.025) shows random batching is moderately stable. In median tier, this ablation is the key evidence that batch composition quality is NOT the bottleneck — merge strategy is.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Per-Type Splitting\",\n    \"description\": \"UniNER-style 1 type per batch. Upper bound on decomposition recall, reveals latency-quality tradeoff.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"per_type_f1\": 0.292,\n      \"per_type_precision\": 0.194,\n      \"per_type_recall\": 0.586,\n      \"per_type_batches\": 66,\n      \"per_type_time_s\": 2532,\n      \"per_type_recall_vs_single\": 0.218,\n      \"per_type_precision_vs_single\": -0.369,\n      \"per_type_f1_vs_single\": -0.153,\n      \"per_type_unique_preds\": 8450,\n      \"single_pass_unique_preds\": 3200,\n      \"merge_dup_rate\": 0.38\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Per-Type Detailed Breakdown\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Single-pass\",\n          \"Per-type\",\n          \"Delta\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"F1\",\n            0.445,\n            0.292,\n            -0.153\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Precision\",\n            0.563,\n            0.194,\n            -0.369\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Recall\",\n            0.368,\n            0.586,\n            0.218\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Unique preds\",\n            3200,\n            8450,\n            \"+164%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Merge duplicates\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"38%\",\n            \"\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Time (s)\",\n            77,\n            2532,\n            \"33x\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Per-type confirms the merge amplification mechanism: recall scales with decomposition granularity but precision degrades faster. The Pareto frontier shows no decomposition point dominates single-pass on F1. This is the strongest evidence for \\\"merge amplification as fundamental bottleneck\\\" — even the oracle partition (zero confusability) cannot beat single-pass without a better merge strategy. Paper uses per-type as the upper bound in the decomposition analysis table.\\n\\n\\nPer-type splitting eliminates ALL type confusion (zero intra-batch confusability) and maximizes recall (0.586 vs single-pass 0.368, +21.8pp). But precision collapses (0.194 vs 0.563, -36.9pp) because: (1) each batch extracts aggressively for its one type — without competing types, the model over-predicts, (2) merging 66 batch outputs creates massive duplication — the same span extracted as different types in different batches, (3) merge heuristics (longest match, confidence-based) cannot reliably disambiguate since each batch's prediction is locally confident. The 38% merge duplicate rate quantifies this directly. Per-type is the theoretical upper bound for recall under decomposition but the worst-case for precision. The 33x latency overhead (2532s vs 77s) makes it impractical regardless. This is already in exp-008 metrics; exp-014 adds systematic analysis of WHY precision drops (merge duplication rate, over-prediction per batch).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SCS Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"spearman_rho_qwen3\": 0.35,\n      \"spearman_p_qwen3\": 0.0001,\n      \"kendall_tau_qwen3\": 0.27,\n      \"spearman_rho_minilm\": 0.28,\n      \"spearman_p_minilm\": 0.001,\n      \"top10_overlap_qwen3\": 0.5,\n      \"top10_overlap_minilm\": 0.4,\n      \"n_pairs_with_empirical\": 420\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: SCS vs Empirical Confusion Rank Correlation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Embedding\",\n          \"Spearman ρ\",\n          \"p\",\n          \"Kendall τ\",\n          \"Top-10 overlap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-32B\",\n            0.35,\n            \"1e-4\",\n            0.27,\n            \"5/10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MiniLM-L6-v2\",\n            0.28,\n            \"1e-3\",\n            0.21,\n            \"4/10\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Top Confused Pairs — Empirical vs Predicted\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Rank\",\n          \"Empirical top pair\",\n          \"Emp. confusion\",\n          \"SCS rank\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            \"person-other ↔ person-politician\",\n            156,\n            2\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            \"person-other ↔ person-artist\",\n            98,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            \"person-athlete ↔ person-other\",\n            87,\n            3\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            \"location-GPE ↔ location-bodiesofwater\",\n            45,\n            18\n          ],\n          [\n            5,\n            \"building-library ↔ building-other\",\n            41,\n            7\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ρ=0.35 validates SCS as a directionally useful proxy — significantly better than random (p<0.001) but insufficient for precise partition optimization. The asymmetry insight (top empirical pair person-other↔politician at SCS rank 2, but location-GPE↔ bodiesofwater at SCS rank 18) suggests type-name semantics capture intra-domain confusion well but miss cross-domain false positives. Paper presents this as motivation for calibration-based approaches and as explaining why SDGD-zero underperforms SDGD-cal. Also explains the D010 embedding sensitivity finding — different embeddings capture different aspects of confusability.\\n\\n\\nEmbedding SCS is a moderate proxy for empirical confusion (ρ=0.35 for Qwen3, 0.28 for MiniLM). The correlation is significant but far from perfect because: (1) embedding similarity captures semantic relatedness, not confusion — \\\"person-artist\\\" and \\\"person-athlete\\\" are semantically similar but the model may distinguish them well from context, (2) empirical confusion is asymmetric (model confuses A→B more than B→A) while cosine similarity is symmetric, (3) empirical confusion is heavily influenced by type frequency and context patterns not captured by type-name embeddings. Qwen3 embeddings outperform MiniLM (ρ=0.35 vs 0.28) on Few-NERD because of extraction-model alignment (consistent with D010 findings). Top-10 overlap at 50% means half the most-confused empirical pairs are also highest-SCS — useful but not sufficient for precision. This validates SCS as a reasonable zero-shot proxy while motivating calibration-based approaches (SDGD-cal). In median tier, this is adequate evidence for \\\"embedding SCS is a useful but imperfect confusability proxy.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD v2 on Few-NERD\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 with merge amplification mitigation (confidence thresholding, duplicate suppression). Tests whether fixing merge recovers SDGD benefit.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_v2_f1\": 0.42,\n      \"sdgd_v2_precision\": 0.44,\n      \"sdgd_v2_recall\": 0.4,\n      \"sdgd_v2_vs_single_delta_f1\": -0.025,\n      \"sdgd_v2_vs_zero_delta_f1\": 0.169,\n      \"sdgd_v2_precision_drop_vs_single\": -0.123,\n      \"merge_dup_rate_v2\": 0.12,\n      \"merge_dup_rate_v1\": 0.38,\n      \"confidence_threshold\": 0.7\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: SDGD v2 vs All Methods\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"P\",\n          \"R\",\n          \"Merge dup%\",\n          \"Time(s)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Single-pass\",\n            0.445,\n            0.563,\n            0.368,\n            \"N/A\",\n            77\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD-zero\",\n            0.251,\n            0.21,\n            0.314,\n            \"38%\",\n            409\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SDGD v2\",\n            0.42,\n            0.44,\n            0.4,\n            \"12%\",\n            450\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random-batch\",\n            0.32,\n            0.249,\n            0.446,\n            \"35%\",\n            476\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SDGD v2 demonstrates that merge amplification IS the bottleneck (fixing it recovers most of the F1 gap) but the fix is incomplete. F1 delta of -2.5pp vs single-pass means SDGD v2 is not cost-effective (5x latency for -2.5pp). Paper pivots: SDGD's primary contribution is diagnostic (merge amplification) rather than practical (improved extraction). The v2 result becomes evidence for \\\"merge amplification is partially addressable but fundamentally limits naive decomposition approaches.\\\" This explains why UniNER and LLM-PEE report modest, inconsistent gains — they also face merge amplification but don't characterize it.\\n\\nSDGD v2 partially mitigates merge amplification through confidence-based deduplication and span-level voting across batches. Merge duplicate rate drops from 38% to 12%. Precision recovers substantially (0.44 vs SDGD-zero's 0.21) but still below single-pass (0.56) because: (1) confidence calibration is imperfect — the model's confidence doesn't reliably distinguish correct from incorrect type assignments, (2) some legitimate multi-type spans get suppressed by dedup, reducing recall slightly, (3) without explicit negative examples per batch, the model still over-extracts for the types it sees. Net F1 is 0.42, within -2.5pp of single-pass — a massive improvement over SDGD-zero but not a clear win. In median tier, this is \\\"marginal or negative\\\" — the delta is within noise for a method that costs 5x more API calls. Paper frames as: \\\"merge mitigation partially recovers decomposition benefit but does not achieve the positive result needed to claim SDGD as a practical method.\\\"\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":794,"startLine":1,"totalLines":794}}}
