Now I'm organizing the tables to structure the experimental evidence: validation results, cross-model comparisons, factorial analysis, and decomposition breakdowns across the different datasets.\n\nWriting the LaTeX structure...\n\nNow I'm mapping out how to frame the results—the pairwise confusability findings from the primary study should anchor the narrative, with the complementary experiments building on those core observations to show the broader patterns across different degradation channels and model architectures. I see the distinction now—experiment_data.json contains the complete dataset for this paper with all 9 experiments that need their own sections and tables, while the framing document provides narrative context and background. The instruction is clear that quantitative claims should be grounded in experiment_data.json, so I should use those 9 experiments as my primary source and treat the framing's mentions of other results as contextual references I can integrate naturally rather than as direct data points. and established results from the framing document as context. I'll prioritize the nine experiments from experiment_data.json as my core findings and only reference the baseline numbers in passing for framing.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the paper. My structure will be: a title framing this as a controlled empirical study of schema degradation in LLM information extraction, an abstract covering the three main contributions, an introduction positioning the problem and existing assumptions, and a related work section on prior approaches like UniNER and LLM-PEE.\n\nFor the methodology, I'm laying out the multi-channel framework that connects count-based metrics to recall and confusability to type confusion, introducing the Schema Confusability Score as an embedding-based proxy, describing the SDGD framework with zero-shot and calibration-based approaches, and detailing the three datasets spanning different scales and task types along with the two mode